{"verdict": "accept", "reason": "The task is ansible.builtin.user with name=service and password_lock=yes. On Debian this runs usermod -L, which puts '!' in front of the shadow hash and disables password authentication. Baseline: the unlocked hash gets locked. Adversarial: the hash already starts with '!', so the module skips -L and it stays locked. Both end states meet the request. The user is left in place and no other account settings are changed."}
